depgraph: mergeable set from the frontier's leaf set, one-node graph removal

Sub-series: B (cycle search and frontier) - patch 6 of 6; applies to 3.0.83 with only the earlier patches of this sub-series.
Unchanged, but patch 0022 before it is now required-use-memo (probe-certain-atoms, the old 0022, is 0029); the step figures below are those of the old order.

The set of mergeable nodes was built as set(frontier_leaves(level)): sort
the leaves, filter them, hash every node again; once per priority range in
every pass. frontier_mergeable() copies the frontier's leaf set and removes
the nodes the filter rejects. Only a node that is not a Package, or is an
uninstall, can be rejected, and the frontier now remembers those few nodes
(`special`), so only they are tested. The copy has the same members but not
the same iteration order as the original set, and that order decides
between candidates for which Package.__lt__ gives no order and is the input
order of sorted() and _gather_deps_closures(). So find_smallest_cycle
searches the least leaf over the unordered intersection only as long as
every comparison is strict (then the result is the minimum in any order);
on the first tie, and before sorted() or the closure pass, it switches to
the originally ordered set, which is built on demand (use_ordered).
This replaces the reduced forms of patches 0003 and 0007 in
find_smallest_cycle.

_serialize_tasks removes one node at a time, and
digraph.difference_update() walks the whole order list and tests every node
of the graph for membership each time. For a set of one node,
_FrontierDigraph now finds the node's position in the order list by
bisection on the frontier's order-indices (they increase along the list),
checks that the entry found is that node, and then unlinks it the way the
generic code does, with a new order list as before. When the position
cannot be confirmed the generic code runs. induced_subgraph() of one node
builds the one-node graph (and its self-edge, if any) directly.

On top of patches 0001-0023, resolve seconds before -> after
(median of the runs, at least 2 per figure):
  start 22.1 -> 20.4, holds 11.5 -> 11.5, masks 23.4 -> 23.3,
  py313 14.0 -> 13.0, bdeps 16.2 -> 16.1, policykit 11.7 -> 11.7
  sum of six 98.8 -> 95.9 (-2.9 s, -0.6 % of the stock sum 447.5)
Patches 0001-0024 together: 447.5 -> 95.9 s (-79 %); 447.5 is the
stock sum of the ladder run of 2026-11-20, stock was not run again here.

Two pieces merged, each too small to stand alone here: directly after 0011
(first order, chain-prelim, single runs) the mergeable set measured 4.1 s
and the one-node removal 3.3 s; at this position they give 2.9 s together.
Kept apart from the leftovers because the ordering fallback is the most
intricate logic of the series after the tracker, and because it carries the
replacement of the reduced forms of 0003 and 0007.

Weak spots and what was not checked:
* Relies on Package.__lt__ being transitive where it gives an order. The
  fallback to the ordered set was not counted, so how often ties occur is
  unknown.
* bisect_left(key=) needs Python 3.10. The shortcut relies on order-indices
  increasing along mygraph.order; the identity check at the found position
  guards it.

Origin: written by AI agents (Claude) in the emerge-fast job, 2026-11-17..20;
isolated from the full patch and measured on 2026-11-20..21. Not a human-written
change. Base: Portage 3.0.83. Test: `emerge -p -uND @world` on six states of
one system image, one resolve at a time; every run gave the same merge list
in the same order, the same number of backtracking attempts and the same
package count as unpatched 3.0.83.
